      "math_academy_id": "q-112634",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14228212/assets/ec193bb78fbfb43cec5266622ef0bef14e6aad5798687ae9c7cc7233e69078cb.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=f(3(x-5))?$",
      "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=f(3(x-5)),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(3)}=\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(3x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14228212/assets/c41d06f741882e3993ebe8eb7eab5c42c362d3c11b9b8ad1e4fed983498c95d0.png)\n\n- Then, translate it right by $5$ units to get the graph of $y=f(3(x-5)).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14228212/assets/47bf38d228b4337b870baf777c53fbab43743f155f4a23aa6d0e2bd1f5bc45ad.png)",
